{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790266237,"nonce":"u0JjXaLh6ZyPEPbQg80/oU8iMF0=","message":"Both limits are lifted, in 66bed6a and a82a3df.\n\n1. Enum construction. A plugin enum's class object answers each fieldless variant by name, and each variant with fields is published as a static function whose target is that variant's core constructor. The plugin publishes these for every language, so Wren writes `Event.RedrawRequested`, `ScaleSize.Physical(640, 480)` or `TextureFormat.Rgba8unorm`, and the core checks each field as for any constructor (`Event.Touch(4, 5, ...)` raises \"window.Event.Touch: invalid phase field\").\n2. Int64. An Int64 that a double holds exactly (any value, not only up to 2^53) enters Wren as a Num. Any other stays the core's box, an instance of a built-in `Int64` class with `toString`, `==(_)` and `!=(_)`, and crosses back as the same integer. No Num can equal such a value, so comparing with a Num is false, as it should be.\n\nplugin_window_events_wren builds a Touch from Wren with nested enums and round-trips a touch id of 2^60 + 1; the interop suite passes, also under ASH_GC_STRESS=1 WLIFT_GC_STRESS=1 for the enum tests.\n\nNoted while testing, not changed:\n- wren_lift's `System.print` and string interpolation format an instance without calling its `toString`; a plain Wren class with `toString { \"probe\" }` prints \"instance of Probe (Instance)\". So `System.print(id)` on an Int64 instance shows the class; `id.toString` gives the digits.\n- Enum values have no equality in Wren: each `Event.Closed` is a fresh value and Wren's `==` on instances is identity, so Wren compares `constructor` or `tag`.\n- The window plugin's `physical_scale_size` and `default_scale_size` factories are no longer needed for Wren.","files":null}]}